WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:01.459
That's true.

00:00:01.459 --> 00:00:02.459
They bought him stuff.

00:00:02.459 --> 00:00:04.080
They bought him stuff, Brian, and that's what
love is.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:05.080
They bought him a lot of stuff.

00:00:05.080 --> 00:00:10.030
I don't know if you know that, but that's
what love is.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:13.320
Nina, what are some of Voldemort's most redeeming
qualities?

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:18.029
Oh, this was a tough question when you asked
me, Brian, because it feels like when people

00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:21.349
say, "At least Hitler was a painter and liked
dogs."

00:00:21.349 --> 00:00:25.380
It was kind of thin on the ground.

00:00:25.380 --> 00:00:32.130
I guess the biggest thing I came up with is
if you know about his backstory, it's very

00:00:32.130 --> 00:00:33.130
troubled.

00:00:33.130 --> 00:00:39.199
I feel like this is mentioned a bit in the
films, but the books definitely go more into

00:00:39.199 --> 00:00:40.199
it.

00:00:40.199 --> 00:00:45.730
Where his mother was a witch who tricked a
handsome Muggle into marrying her, and then

00:00:45.730 --> 00:00:47.320
used love potion.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.039
They had a child, he left her.

00:00:51.039 --> 00:00:55.470
Yeah, and then the mother dies, and so he's
left at an orphanage and then brought to Hogwarts

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.440
later.

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:03.280
There is a horrible, tragic quality to that
whole story that he's born to people who do

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:06.759
not love each other outside of magical artifice.

00:01:06.759 --> 00:01:09.799
His mother does love him, but dies pretty
quickly.

00:01:09.799 --> 00:01:12.570
I mean, there is a sympathetic...

00:01:12.570 --> 00:01:15.560
Oh God, it feels so gross to say that, edge
to Voldemort.

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:16.560
Just say it.

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:17.560
Just say it.

00:01:17.560 --> 00:01:18.560
It's true.

00:01:18.560 --> 00:01:19.560
Fine, I'm going to own it.

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:20.560
There's a sympathetic element to his character.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:21.560
He had mommy issues.

00:01:21.560 --> 00:01:22.560
Yeah.

00:01:22.560 --> 00:01:23.719
He has parent issues across the board.

00:01:23.719 --> 00:01:24.719
Who doesn't?

00:01:24.719 --> 00:01:30.920
He is also an orphan, but his parents... at
least Harry's parents really liked each other.

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:34.659
I don't think Voldemort's tolerated each other
outside of the fact that his mom was drugging

00:01:34.659 --> 00:01:36.479
his dad on a regular basis.

00:01:36.479 --> 00:01:37.790
I mean, that's just...

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.040
I mean, that's typical these days, I think.

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:41.539
I mean, that is the plot of Phantom Thread.

00:01:41.539 --> 00:01:44.840
Sorry to spoil that film for anyone who hasn't
watched it.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.789
I guess, it's an immortal...

00:01:46.789 --> 00:01:50.570
There's a lot of crossover between the Harry
Potter crowd and the Phantom Thread crowd.

00:01:50.570 --> 00:01:51.700
Yeah, probably.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:52.700
Yeah.

00:01:52.700 --> 00:01:53.700
I would think so.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:54.700
Yeah.

00:01:54.700 --> 00:01:59.150
Yeah, so I think that's the biggest thing
I came up with is that there is a very tragic,

00:01:59.150 --> 00:02:03.899
sad element to him and I think that needs
to be considered, but also does not excuse

00:02:03.899 --> 00:02:05.049
anything that he ever does.

00:02:05.049 --> 00:02:07.700
I need to be very clear about that.

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:14.340
For you, the redeeming quality is that he's
got a backstory that a person can relate to.

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:15.340
Yeah.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:20.350
We all come from our own version of that,
and it's not necessarily that, but no one's

00:02:20.350 --> 00:02:21.750
childhood is idyllic.

00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:26.360
No one has perfect parents, no one has a perfect
family.

00:02:26.360 --> 00:02:27.360
Right.

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:33.010
When you consider his backstory, you can start
to see why he is the way he is?

00:02:33.010 --> 00:02:37.670
He was a lonely abandoned kid, orphaned kid
who grew up with nothing, and when you look

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:42.491
at another antagonist like Draco Malfoy, who
grew up in a mansion with two loving parents.

00:02:42.491 --> 00:02:43.520
Rich kid.

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:45.880
Really every amount of-

00:02:45.880 --> 00:02:46.880
Loving parents?

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:47.880
Loving?

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:48.880
Well, liking.

00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:50.660
They loved him enough to get his hair done
so it matched.

00:02:50.660 --> 00:02:51.660
Yeah.

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:52.660
They bought him stuff.

00:02:52.660 --> 00:02:54.250
They bought him stuff, Brian, and that's what
love is.

00:02:54.250 --> 00:02:55.250
They bought him a lot of stuff.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:58.580
I don't know if you know that, but that's
what love is.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:02:59.580
I hope you're right.

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:05.570
That kid had every element of privilege and
still just sucked.

00:03:05.570 --> 00:03:07.020
Yeah.

00:03:07.020 --> 00:03:13.940
There's a sympathetic element to Voldemort's
entire backstory, and that's kind of all I've

00:03:13.940 --> 00:03:15.490
got about the guy.

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:16.490
That's something.

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:17.490
Yeah.

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:18.490
It's a thing.

00:03:18.490 --> 00:03:19.490
It's terrific.

00:03:19.490 --> 00:03:22.870
I think you could have gotten him out on parole.

00:03:22.870 --> 00:03:23.870
Yeah.

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:28.460
I'll see if he's up in 10 or 20 years.

00:03:28.460 --> 00:03:30.060
Tom, what about you?

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:34.630
What are some of Voldemort's most redeeming
qualities that you could think of?

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:41.150
You know, I was thinking about it and I think
that he, is aside from the very important

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:48.820
detail of his being British, I think that
he is the most American villain that you could

00:03:48.820 --> 00:03:49.820
ask for.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:54.620
I think he's got this kind of an idealized
version of Reagan-era politics to him where

00:03:54.620 --> 00:03:59.290
he picked himself up by his bootstraps, lifted
himself up off the ground.

00:03:59.290 --> 00:04:01.200
He gets done with school, what does he do?

00:04:01.200 --> 00:04:04.260
Does he dwell on the fact that, yeah, he killed
some kids?

00:04:04.260 --> 00:04:05.260
He doesn't.

00:04:05.260 --> 00:04:10.120
He goes out there, and after getting, if I'm
not mistaken, Nina, he was offered a job in

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:11.120
government, right?

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:15.180
He was offered a job either at Hogwarts or
in the Ministry, I'm not sure which, and he

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:16.180
takes a job at a store.

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.139
At a small business, right?

00:04:19.139 --> 00:04:25.939
Supporting a small business, and then he uses
that to build himself up further, right?

00:04:25.939 --> 00:04:28.229
That's where he learns about Horcruxes and
stuff, right?

00:04:28.229 --> 00:04:30.520
No, he knew about... no, no, Tom.

00:04:30.520 --> 00:04:31.850
He knew about Horcruxes before that.

00:04:31.850 --> 00:04:35.310
It's where he finds out where the most valuable
items aren't going so he can make them into

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:36.730
Horcruxes.

00:04:36.730 --> 00:04:42.000
That's even better, so he's got an eye for
business, and then he starts his own small

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:44.210
business from the ground up.

00:04:44.210 --> 00:04:46.090
He inspires love in his followers.

00:04:46.090 --> 00:04:49.150
Are you calling the Death Eaters a small business?

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:50.150
It's a catchy name.

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:51.150
Yeah, I mean what is it if it's not?

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:52.150
It's good branding.

00:04:52.150 --> 00:04:53.150
Terrific name, yeah.

00:04:53.150 --> 00:04:55.909
What income do they make?

00:04:55.909 --> 00:04:56.909
I don't know.

00:04:56.909 --> 00:04:57.909
I mean, there's friendship.

00:04:57.909 --> 00:04:58.909
Finders keepers.

00:04:58.909 --> 00:04:59.909
The greatest treasure of all.

00:04:59.909 --> 00:05:01.990
That's a great point, Brian.

00:05:01.990 --> 00:05:04.260
Whatever they finds, they keeps.

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:05.620
You're right.

00:05:05.620 --> 00:05:12.090
If they had spelled Death Eaters wrong, as
one word, it would have been a great app startup.

00:05:12.090 --> 00:05:15.210
No, so I think that there are a lot of good
qualities in the guy.

00:05:15.210 --> 00:05:20.270
I think he's economical in the... there's
that old, what's that journalistic phrase

00:05:20.270 --> 00:05:24.090
where you never use a 5 cent word or never
used a 10 cent word when a 5 cent word will

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:29.610
do, and he brings that to his face where he
doesn't need the hair.

00:05:29.610 --> 00:05:30.910
What's the nose doing for it?

00:05:30.910 --> 00:05:31.910
Let's lose it.

00:05:31.910 --> 00:05:33.069
Lose it.

00:05:33.069 --> 00:05:34.249
Lose it.

00:05:34.249 --> 00:05:35.879
It's just wind resistance.

00:05:35.879 --> 00:05:37.500
Get out of here.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:38.500
Yeah.

00:05:38.500 --> 00:05:39.500
Yeah.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:42.159
There's another old truism, "A design is not
perfect when there's nothing left to add,

00:05:42.159 --> 00:05:45.659
but rather when there's nothing left to take
away" and that's what he did with the front

00:05:45.659 --> 00:05:46.659
of his head.

00:05:46.659 --> 00:05:52.060
Tom, would you also argue that he's persistent,
and that's a good quality because every single

00:05:52.060 --> 00:05:53.819
year he tries to kill a teenager?

00:05:53.819 --> 00:05:54.819
Absolutely.

00:05:54.819 --> 00:05:56.050
Actually, I have one more.

00:05:56.050 --> 00:05:59.550
He's considerate because when he tries to
kill that teenager every year, he does it

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:02.780
right after the school year is ending, or
when it's about to end.

00:06:02.780 --> 00:06:05.490
He is very considerate of Harry's schedule.

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:06.960
He makes sure they get through finals.

00:06:06.960 --> 00:06:07.960
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:10.849
He wants to make sure the kids get their learning
done first.

00:06:10.849 --> 00:06:13.719
He's got the summer blahs at the beginning
of the year.

00:06:13.719 --> 00:06:14.719
Yeah.

00:06:14.719 --> 00:06:19.321
Lets them get past that, really refined himself,
redefined himself in a lot of ways.

00:06:19.321 --> 00:06:22.490
He always waits till May to attack and I just
think that's admirable.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:26.400
Well, who wants to start a magic war in the
middle of winter?

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:27.400
True.

00:06:27.400 --> 00:06:28.400
We're all down.

00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:29.400
Yeah.

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:30.529
It's so gray outside.

00:06:30.529 --> 00:06:33.289
You want to make sure that the days are as
long as possible.

00:06:33.289 --> 00:06:35.150
Absolutely, get that sunshine.

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:38.150
Get that vitamin D.

00:06:38.150 --> 00:06:41.150
If there's one thing he's known for it's for
soaking up those rays.

00:06:41.150 --> 00:06:42.479
He's got such a great complexion.

00:06:42.479 --> 00:06:45.360
Yeah, Voldemort is such a sun bunny.

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:52.439
He put out that surf album that was pretty
good right out of college when he was still

00:06:52.439 --> 00:06:53.439
finding himself.

00:06:53.439 --> 00:06:54.439
Beach Boy covers.

00:06:54.439 --> 00:06:55.439
Yeah.

00:06:55.439 --> 00:06:56.439
Yeah.

00:06:56.439 --> 00:06:57.439
It's really good.

00:06:57.439 --> 00:07:00.059
He probably saved a lot of money by rooming
with Professor Quirrell in the first book

00:07:00.059 --> 00:07:02.090
by just living on the back of his head.

00:07:02.090 --> 00:07:03.340
The guy's got, he's admirable.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:04.800
That was mine actually...

00:07:04.800 --> 00:07:10.009
That was similar to mine because I was going
to say he's so, he's single-minded, he's dedicated.

00:07:10.009 --> 00:07:11.009
Yeah.

00:07:11.009 --> 00:07:16.710
This is a guy who is committed, so committed,
that he'll do anything he needs to do to achieve

00:07:16.710 --> 00:07:22.139
his goals, including sticking himself on the
back of someone's head that is also wrapped

00:07:22.139 --> 00:07:25.610
up in a tight turban, so he's not getting
any-

00:07:25.610 --> 00:07:30.039
No, the turban is specifically to hide him
though.

00:07:30.039 --> 00:07:32.080
That's the only option.

00:07:32.080 --> 00:07:33.080
That's what I'm saying.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:34.080
That's what I'm saying.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:35.080
That's how into...

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:38.650
He's like, "Whatever, just stick me on the
back of your head, wear a turban.

00:07:38.650 --> 00:07:40.139
It's the only way that this will work.

00:07:40.139 --> 00:07:42.690
I don't care that I can't breathe or see."

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:43.690
Is that how you think the conversation went?

00:07:43.690 --> 00:07:46.870
He just walked up to this guy and he was like,
"Hey, stick me on the back of your head,"

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:48.240
and the guy went, "Absolutely."

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:50.419
Guys, I got to stop you right here.

00:07:50.419 --> 00:07:52.580
Okay, there's a couple of things.

00:07:52.580 --> 00:07:54.930
First of all, the turban was not the only
option.

00:07:54.930 --> 00:07:57.449
Let me just give the Photoshop team headaches
here.

00:07:57.449 --> 00:08:03.120
He could have worn a backwards baseball cap
that would have looked pretty fly, I think

00:08:03.120 --> 00:08:04.120
you'll agree.

00:08:04.120 --> 00:08:07.320
Secondly, Brian, I don't think you get to
call somebody single-minded if they're literally

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:08.879
living on somebody else's brain.

00:08:08.879 --> 00:08:09.879
Okay.

00:08:09.879 --> 00:08:10.879
Fair enough.

00:08:10.879 --> 00:08:11.879
Fair point.

00:08:11.879 --> 00:08:12.879
That's true.

00:08:12.879 --> 00:08:15.259
Another hat option to celebrate the second
season of the greatest show of all time is

00:08:15.259 --> 00:08:20.210
that he could have worn a fedora with a safari
brim down the back, like Tim Robinson in I

00:08:20.210 --> 00:08:21.379
Think You Should Leave.

00:08:21.379 --> 00:08:23.499
Everyone loved that hat.

00:08:23.499 --> 00:08:26.270
He's carrying around dice in his robes.

00:08:26.270 --> 00:08:27.270
Yeah.

00:08:27.270 --> 00:08:28.729
It would be a great look for him.

00:08:28.729 --> 00:08:30.080
I like it.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:32.801
So, Brian, yours is his persistence and his
single-mindedness.

00:08:32.801 --> 00:08:33.801
Yeah.

00:08:33.801 --> 00:08:38.360
I just was going to say, this is a guy who
will do what it takes.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:44.780
If that means making it so that he can't breathe
and all he can smell is some guy's head at

00:08:44.780 --> 00:08:47.960
all times, at all hours.

00:08:47.960 --> 00:08:51.090
How does... does he eat?

00:08:51.090 --> 00:08:54.630
Does Quirrell put the soup spoon behind his
head?

00:08:54.630 --> 00:08:56.460
Oh, I never thought of that.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.780
Does he just let rats crawl up his back so
that he can munch on them or something?

00:09:00.780 --> 00:09:02.020
I don't know how to-

00:09:02.020 --> 00:09:03.880
He lets Pettigrew crawl up probably.

00:09:03.880 --> 00:09:05.430
His henchman is a rat.

00:09:05.430 --> 00:09:06.910
They do something else, I think.

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:09.120
That scene would have been so great in the
movies.

00:09:09.120 --> 00:09:15.160
You get to see him at the great hall at dinner
and he's sneaking a straw up into the back.

00:09:15.160 --> 00:09:17.310
He's pushing a chicken nugget in the back
of his turban.

00:09:17.310 --> 00:09:21.390
No, he has a chicken, a full drum stick and
he's just scratching the back of his head

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:22.390
with it.

00:09:22.390 --> 00:09:25.310
Pulls out a bone and he's like, "Oh, that's
weird."

00:09:25.310 --> 00:09:26.310
Oh, magic.

00:09:26.310 --> 00:09:27.310
Magic.

00:09:27.310 --> 00:09:28.820
Yeah, and nobody thinks it's weird because
magic.

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:29.820
Yeah.

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:32.670
You can get away with a lot at Hogwarts because
magic.

00:09:32.670 --> 00:09:33.670
Because magic.

00:09:33.670 --> 00:09:34.670
Yeah.

00:09:34.670 --> 00:09:35.670
So, yeah, that's my pick.

00:09:35.670 --> 00:09:41.680
I think if you want something done, he may
not actually get it done, but he will do everything

00:09:41.680 --> 00:09:43.500
he can to try.

00:09:43.500 --> 00:09:45.510
You bet he will, short of trimming his nails.

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:46.690
Short of trimming his nails.

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:48.100
I mean, that's a bridge too far.

00:09:48.100 --> 00:09:51.930
I agree with you that he is single-minded
Brian, but I do want to push back against

00:09:51.930 --> 00:09:53.450
that he'll do whatever it takes.

00:09:53.450 --> 00:09:57.510
Because if you think about the plan he comes
up with in Goblet of Fire, his whole plan

00:09:57.510 --> 00:10:01.710
is have a henchman sneak into Hogwarts.

00:10:01.710 --> 00:10:04.400
Harry has to live through all of these super
dangerous tasks.

00:10:04.400 --> 00:10:07.730
He cannot die during any of them, or the whole
plan is ruined.

00:10:07.730 --> 00:10:08.760
Okay.

00:10:08.760 --> 00:10:16.840
No one else can win the tournament, and this
is like a nine to ten month gambit where literally

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:20.200
everything could go wrong at every second,
just for a vial of blood.

00:10:20.200 --> 00:10:25.660
He could have just been like, "Hey, rat, henchman,
go bite him in his sleep and put it in a vial

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:26.660
and come back."

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:29.170
I mean, they could have killed him dozens
of times.

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:30.170
Yeah.

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:31.170
In dozens of different ways.

00:10:31.170 --> 00:10:36.490
Voldemort is very, it's very like Dr. Evil
vibes where he lowers them over a pool of

00:10:36.490 --> 00:10:37.490
sharks with laser beams.

00:10:37.490 --> 00:10:38.490
Sharks with lasers, yeah.

00:10:38.490 --> 00:10:39.840
How will you get out of this?

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:40.840
Yeah.

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:43.880
Voldemort does have that vibe a little bit,
but Goblet is the worst example to me because

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:49.120
I'm like, this is a full year's worth of nonsense
that could all go very, very, very wrong,

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:52.490
and yet this is your grand plan.

00:10:52.490 --> 00:10:54.540
There were definitely easier ways to do this.

00:10:54.540 --> 00:10:57.690
He's from the mouse trap school of plans.

00:10:57.690 --> 00:10:58.690
Okay.

00:10:58.690 --> 00:11:00.130
Now I'm in this.

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:04.620
He needs to make sure that the boot has to
kick it in just the right way.

00:11:04.620 --> 00:11:05.620
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:11:05.620 --> 00:11:06.620
You have to get...

00:11:06.620 --> 00:11:07.640
The gear has to be just in place.

00:11:07.640 --> 00:11:08.640
Yeah.

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:09.640
Yeah.

00:11:09.640 --> 00:11:16.580
So Harry, once this man dives into a small
cup of water, I think you'll find...

